> I note that this isn't so much a "queer" patch as a bisexual patch.

I was under the impression that "queer" is a blanket term for all things
non-heterosexual. I was using it in much the same was as one would use
LGBT (an acronym for lesbian, gay, bi, and transgendered). I learned this
terminology from the internet so I may be wrong, though.

> Also it alters the balance of the game, given the advantages that a
> knowlegeable player can get from consorting with foocubi:  s/he has
> twice as many opportunities to gain levels and such.

I guess I should have addressed this in the announcement. Version 2 uses
the same trick as the original Queer Patch to maintain game balance, at
least as far as it is possible. When the queer option is set, foocubi are
twice as likely to develop a severe headache after an encounter. This
means that for players who choose to be bisexual, the average number of
encounters over the course of the game will be about the same as in
vanilla NetHack. Players who adhere to the straight or gay #conducts will
have only half the opportunities for level gains and so on.

> I might suggest straight, gay, and bi; and to maintain something close to
> the original game balance, bi characters should find themselves with half
> of all foocubi cancelled on appearance.

Version 1 of the patch had an 'orientation' option which could be set to
straight, gay, or bi. I changed the patch because people in the newsgroup
convinced me that the player shouldn't get to decide what happens with
magical seduction.  If people prefer it I could make both versions
available on my web page. Cast your votes, everybody.

> Furthermore, there's the amulet of change and polymorph.  So in Nethack
> there are *five* possible sexual orientations:  opposite sex, same sex,
> male only, female only, and bi.

The player can always run away from the foocubi they don't like and
maintain whatever sexual orientation they like. Are you saying you would
like me to add conducts for "only slept with men" and "only slept with
women?"

